roups.com</a> [mailto:<a rel=\"nofollow\">geomorph-...@googlegroups.com</a>]\r\n<b>On Behalf Of </b>Thomas Richardson<br>\r\n<b>Sent:</b> Thursday, March 1, 2018 11:26 AM<br>\r\n<b>To:</b> geomorph R package &lt;<a rel=\"nofollow\">geomorph-...@googlegroups.com</a>&gt;<br>\r\n<b>Subject:</b> [geomorph-r-package] DA and asymetric components from bilat.symmetry</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"> </p>\r\n<div>\r\n<div>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Hi all, </p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"> </p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">I used the bilat.symmetry function my dataset (53 landmarks, 688 individual) and got the DA component, but it has the dimensions (53,2,2). What does this represent? I would have thought it would give me some sort of output showing the average\r\n deviation of each point from it&#39;s paired point, and the average deviation of the unpaired points from the median line, but that wouldn&#39;t account for the 3rd dimension?</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"> </p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Also, what exactly is the asymmetric component of variation? I&#39;ve read multiple sources but still don&#39;t understand - I get that the symmetric component is just a symmetrical version of the face, but what is the asymmetric component?</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"> </p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Cheers,</p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"> </p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Richie</p>\r\n</div>\r\n</div>\r\n</div></div></blockquote></div></div>"
