font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif;color:#1f497d\"><u></u><u></u></span></span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif;color:#1f497d\">phone: <a href=\"tel:(515)%20294-3834\" value=\"+15152943834\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\">515-294-3834</a><u></u><u></u></span></span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif;color:#1f497d\"><u></u> <u></u></span></span></p>\r\n<span></span>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\">From:</span></b><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\"> <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">geomorph-...@googlegroups.com</a> [mailto:<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">geomorph-...@googlegroups.com</a>]\r\n<b>On Behalf Of </b>Thomas Richardson<br>\r\n<b>Sent:</b> Friday, March 2, 2018 7:28 AM<br>\r\n<b>To:</b> geomorph R package &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">geomorph-...@googlegroups.com</a>&gt;<br>\r\n<b>Subject:</b> Re: [geomorph-r-package] DA and asymetric components from bilat.symmetry<u></u><u></u></span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p>\r\n</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div lang=\"EN-US\" link=\"blue\" vlink=\"purple\"><div><div>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Hi Dean,<u></u><u></u></p>\r\n<div>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">for DA component the help file reads &quot;The directional asymmetry component, found as the mean shape for each side.&quot; I don&#39;t see how this corresponds to a 53 x 2 x 2 dataset. I get that the second and 3rd dimension are x,y and face side respectively,\r\n but each side of the face doesn&#39;t have 53 landmarks&gt;? <u></u><u></u></p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Sorry, I I think I asked a different question to the one I wanted an answer for. The asymmetric component of variation is well described in the literature, but what isn&#39;t clear to me is how it relates to the output of the bilat.symmetry function.\r\n The symmetric component seems to be the aligned coordinates after the face has been made symmetrical, but I don&#39;t understand what the coordinates given by the asymmetric component represent?<u></u><u></u></p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p>\r\n</div>\r\n</div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div lang=\"EN-US\" link=\"blue\" vlink=\"purple\"><div><div><div>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Richie<br>\r\n<br>\r\nOn Thursday, March 1, 2018 at 5:40:24 PM UTC, Dean Adams wrote:<u></u><u></u></p>\r\n</div></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div lang=\"EN-US\" link=\"blue\" vlink=\"purple\"><div><div><div><blockquote style=\"border:none;border-left:solid #cccccc 1.0pt;padding:0in 0in 0in 6.0pt;margin-left:4.8pt;margin-right:0in\"><div><div>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><a name=\"m_-9083750703713100861_MWHPR04MB0559AE582569F879212495E6A2C60@M\" rel=\"nofollow\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif;color:#1f497d\">Richie,\r\n</span></a><u></u><u></u></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif;color:#1f497d\"> </span><u></u><u></u></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif;color:#1f497d\">Please read the help file for the function a bit more carefully. Both of those are clearly described.\r\n</span><u></u><u></u></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif;color:#1f497d\"> </span><u></u><u></u></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif;color:#1f497d\">As to what the asymmetric component of shape variation represents, I suggest a read of the primary\r\n literature for that. It is explained in numerous publications on symmetry analysis of shape data.\r\n</span><u></u><u></u></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif;color:#1f497d\"> </span><u></u><u></u></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif;color:#1f497d\">Dean</span><u></u><u></u></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif;color:#1f497d\"> </span><u></u><u></u></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif;color:#1f497d\">Dr. Dean C. Adams</span><u></u><u></u></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif;color:#1f497d\">Director of Graduate Education, EEB Program</span><u></u><u></u></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif;color:#1f497d\">Professor</span><u></u><u></u></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif;color:#1f497d\">Department of Ecology, Evolution, and Organismal Biology</span><u></u><u></u></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif;color:#1f497d\">       Department of Statistics</span><u></u><u></u></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif;color:#1f497d\">Iowa State University</span><u></u><u></u></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><a href=\"http://www.public.iastate.edu/~dcadams/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.public.iastate.edu/~dcadams/&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792485844326000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2GtL3KPX-xqfjo_SjdQ6b7\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif;color:purple\">www.public.iastate.edu/~dcadams/</span></a><u></u><u></u></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif;color:#1f497d\">phone: <a href=\"tel:(515)%20294-3834\" value=\"+15152943834\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\">515-294-3834</a></span><u></u><u></u></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif;color:#1f497d\"> </span><u></u><u></u></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\">From:</span></b><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\">\r\n<a rel=\"nofollow\">geomorph-...@googlegroups.com</a> [mailto:<a rel=\"nofollow\">geomorph-...@googlegroups.com</a>]\r\n<b>On Behalf Of </b>Thomas Richardson<br>\r\n<b>Sent:</b> Thursday, March 1, 2018 11:26 AM<br>\r\n<b>To:</b> geomorph R package &lt;<a rel=\"nofollow\">geomorph-...@googlegroups.com</a>&gt;<br>\r\n<b>Subject:</b> [geomorph-r-package] DA and asymetric components from bilat.symmetry</span><u></u><u></u></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"> <u></u><u></u></p>\r\n<div>\r\n<div>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Hi all, <u></u><u></u></p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"> <u></u><u></u></p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">I used the bilat.symmetry function my dataset (53 landmarks, 688 individual) and got the DA component, but it has the dimensions (53,2,2). What does this represent? I would have\r\n thought it would give me some sort of output showing the average deviation of each point from it&#39;s paired point, and the average deviation of the unpaired points from the median line, but that wouldn&#39;t account for the 3rd dimension?<u></u><u></u></p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"> <u></u><u></u></p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Also, what exactly is the asymmetric component of variation? I&#39;ve read multiple sources but still don&#39;t understand - I get that the symmetric component is just a symmetrical version\r\n of the face, but what is the asymmetric component?<u></u><u></u></p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"> <u></u><u></u></p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Cheers,<u></u><u></u></p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"> <u></u><u></u></p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Richie<u></u><u></u></p>\r\n</div>\r\n</div>\r\n</div></div></blockquote></div></div></div></div>"
